# Feature-selection run folders are NOT ignored — every run is committed on purpose
# (2026-08-09). The old `reports/feature_selection/*/` rule was dropped because it
# silently hid each new run until someone remembered `git add -f`, and the archive is
# the evidence behind CONTEXT §6-§14.
#
# ⚠️ Dropping that rule was NOT enough, and the 384 files already in the index hid it:
# a tracked file ignores .gitignore, so the archive looked committed while a NEW run's
# `outstanding.csv` and `feature_importance.csv` were still caught by the blanket
# `*.csv` (line 50) and any `*.txt` by line 74. These negations must come AFTER both —
# the last matching rule wins — and they re-include the whole tree, so `figures/*.png`,
# `metadata.json` and `README.md` stay covered too.
!reports/feature_selection/
!reports/feature_selection/**
#
# ⚠️ **ONE QUARANTINED ROOT, ADDED 2026-08-14, AND IT NEEDS ITS OWN PAIR — WHICH IS THE
# WHOLE ARGUMENT AGAINST EXTRA ROOTS, PAID ONCE ON PURPOSE.**
# `reports/feature_selection__forex_return_5day/` holds ONE run: `pool__forex` against
# `return_5day` at d20/h5, the first selection in this repo to measure a bar on a return
# target (`evidence=failed_null`, z = +0.12, p = 0.524).
#
# It is not in the root above because `final_features` groups by (schema, target, setup)
# with no term for which pools: it is the only `return_5day` run in the archive, so in
# the main root it would form a fresh group whose table name is
# `unified_schema_vcb.return_5day__final__d20_h5` — a table that already exists with 754
# columns — and propose rebuilding it from 44 forex channels (the STL-1 domino).
#
# ⚠️ **RETIRE THIS PAIR when either is true**: a `--scope`-named build makes the
# collision impossible, or the orphan 754-column table is dropped. Until then, without
# these two lines the run's CSVs fall straight back into the blanket `*.csv` on line 50
# and the evidence is lost the way issue RPR-1 lost 29 run folders.
!reports/feature_selection__forex_return_5day/
!reports/feature_selection__forex_return_5day/**
#
# ⚠️ A LOOSE FILE DIRECTLY UNDER `reports/` NEEDS ITS OWN NEGATION TOO — the two pairs
# above re-include SUBTREES, and neither matches a file sitting beside them. Added
# 2026-08-14 for `forex_exchanges.csv`, a generated inventory of the 48 forex exchanges
# (see `orchestration/CONTEXT.md`). Without this line the blanket `*.csv` on line 50
# takes it and `git add` reports success while committing nothing — the exact shape of
# issue GIT-1.
!reports/*.csv
#
# ⚠️ **AND THE RANKER EVALUATION NEEDS ITS OWN PAIR TOO** (added 2026-08-16). The
# `reports/ranker_evaluation/` folder is what `python -m feature_selection.ranker_eval`
# writes — `scorecard.csv`, `advantage.csv`, `runtime_shares.csv`, `cost.csv` — and it is
# the evidence behind `feature_selection/RANKER_COMPARISON.md` and CONTEXT §19, i.e. the
# measurement that removed three of six rankers. `git check-ignore` confirms the blanket
# `*.csv` on line 50 takes every one of those files, so without this pair `git add` would
# report success and commit an `evaluation.json` with no numbers beside it: issue GIT-1
# exactly, one root later.
#
# ⚠️ It is a SEPARATE ROOT from `reports/feature_selection/` on purpose and must stay one:
# `contract.run_folders` calls any directory holding a `metadata.json` a selection run, so
# an evaluation archived in there would enter `final_features.plan_from_reports` as a run
# whose channels belong in a `__final__` table. (The writer also names its record
# `evaluation.json` rather than `metadata.json`, so the two guards are independent.)
!reports/ranker_evaluation/
!reports/ranker_evaluation/**
#
# ⚠️ **AND THE THREE ROOTS ADDED 2026-08-19/20 NEEDED THEIR OWN PAIRS — MEASURED 2026-08-20,
# AFTER TWO COMMITS HAD ALREADY SHIPPED WITHOUT THEM.** `PRB-1` split the report tree into
# three roots (a run that measures the SELECTION is not a run that feeds the CHAIN) and
# `SSK-1` added a fourth, and NOT ONE of them got a negation pair. The blanket `*.csv` on
# line 50 took every one of their CSVs, so `git add -A` reported success while committing
# the README, the figures and `metadata.json` and silently dropping `outstanding.csv`,
# `feature_importance.csv`, `validation.csv` and the rest:
#
#   reports/feature_selection_wide/   8 CSVs on disk,  0 tracked
#   reports/feature_selection_vn30/  18 CSVs on disk,  0 tracked
#   reports/feature_selection_probes/ 32 CSVs on disk, 16 tracked (two runs force-added)
#
# ⚠️ `_wide` is the one that bites hardest: RUNBOOK §3b promotes it to a CHAIN INPUT, and
# `final_features --root ../reports/feature_selection_wide` reads exactly the
# `outstanding.csv` that was not being committed — so a fresh clone plans nothing and says
# nothing, which is `plan_from_reports` skipping a folder with no `outstanding.csv`, the
# silent failure CLAUDE.md §3b already records.
#
# ⚠️ **This is the FOURTH time this exact shape has been paid** (GIT-1, the forex root, the
# ranker root, now three at once). The lesson the earlier comments drew — "the whole argument
# against extra roots, paid once on purpose" — is now paid four times: **a new report root is
# not free, and its cost is a negation pair remembered at creation time.**
!reports/feature_selection_probes/
!reports/feature_selection_probes/**
!reports/feature_selection_wide/
!reports/feature_selection_wide/**
!reports/feature_selection_vn30/
!reports/feature_selection_vn30/**
# ⚠️ A FIFTH ROOT, 2026-08-21, and its pair was added IN THE SAME COMMIT as the root —
# which is the only reason it is not a sixth instance of the shape above. `_wide` already
# holds PRF-9's h=20 run and `final_features` scopes EVERY table in a plan, so putting an
# h=10 run beside it would have built a junk `rank_20day__final__d20_h20__wide10` duplicate
# of a table that already exists. A separate root is the fix; the negation is its cost.
!reports/feature_selection_wide_h10/
!reports/feature_selection_wide_h10/**
#
# ⚠️ A SIXTH ROOT, 2026-08-28, and the shape was paid a FIFTH time before anyone noticed:
# `reports/pdf_ocr/` was committed WITHOUT its pair, so every run folder went in holding
# `metadata.json` and its per-document JSON and **no `summary.csv`** — the blanket `*.csv`
# above ate it, silently, exactly as the comments above predict. It is not a selection root
# and `final_features` never reads it; it holds `web_scraper.pdf_ocr_job` runs, whose
# `summary.csv` is the one file a reader opens first.
!reports/pdf_ocr/
!reports/pdf_ocr/**
#
# ⚠️ ONE ROOT, and only one, since 2026-08-10. `reports/feature_selection_basic/`,
# `_economy/` and `_superseded/` were three MORE paths these two rules did not match,
# each needing its own negation pair or its CSVs fell straight back into the blanket
# `*.csv`. They were merged into the single root above and all 22 archived runs were
# deleted, so the pair above is again the whole rule.
#
# ⚠️ What those roots bought is now bought by `--scope`, and it is NOT automatic.
# `final_features` groups every run under one root by (schema, target, setup) — a key
# with no term for "which pools" — and every entry point now seeds at 18, so a
# `pool__basic` run and a `basic+economy_<country>` run in this root are ONE group and
# are UNIONED into `return_5day__final__d20_h5`. Name the feature block per build
# (`--scope basic`, `--scope economy_japan`) or accept that union on purpose. If a run
# is ever REDONE, delete or move the superseded folder out of `reports/` — it would
# otherwise be counted as a second contribution to its replacement's table.
#
# ⚠️ They are not small. The deleted archive was ~107 MB across 22 runs, and `channel_correlation.csv`
# alone reached 40.0 MB on a 1,458-channel pool — it is the full N×N pairwise matrix.
# Before committing a new WIDE-pool run, check that one file: the deliverables are
# `feature_importance.csv` and `outstanding.csv`, and `dropped_for` already records
# what each channel was pruned into.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⚠️ `P1-8` / `WFO-1` + `RPR-1`, 2026-08-21 — THE WALK-FORWARD TRACKS WERE
# SIMULTANEOUSLY UNTRACKED AND ONE OMITTED FLAG FROM BEING OVERWRITTEN.
# Measured before writing this: `git ls-files results/` returned NOTHING. The
# blanket `*.csv` on line 50 takes every artefact `walkforward` writes, and
# `results/` — unlike `reports/`, which has negations — was never given any. So
# the repo's strongest evidence (PRF-1 at h=20 and the h=10 track, ~33 GPU-min
# each) existed only on this machine. The refusal in `walkforward/manifest.py`
# fixes the overwrite half; this pair fixes the untracked half.
#
# ⚠️ `predictions_oos.csv` IS DELIBERATELY NOT NEGATED, and the number is why:
# 18 of them total **323 MB** (16.5-19 MB each), against **19 KB** for all 18
# `folds.csv` and **22 KB** for all 8 `per_fold.csv`. `per_fold.csv` carries the
# per-fold Sharpe/IC/CAGR series every register quotes and `folds.csv` carries
# the fold geometry and run names, so the pair below tracks the EVIDENCE at
# 41 KB. The raw OOS predictions stay reproducible-only, which is `RPR-1`
# accepted on purpose rather than discovered later — re-deriving one costs the
# ~33 GPU-minutes recorded in `walkforward/CONTEXT.md`.
#
# `manifest.json` needs no rule: no blanket `*.json` exists, so the identity
# that makes a track re-identifiable is already tracked. Verified with
# `git check-ignore -v`.
!results/**/folds.csv
!results/**/per_fold.csv

reports/pdf_ocr/